# Speech programme

This rule applies **only** when the task is part of the speech programme: the owner dragged a
`docs/SESSION-SPEECH/*.md` file into the chat, or the request names a "Speech phase". Ignore it otherwise.

**Authority.** The owner's decisions D1 to D14 of 17 October 2026 (completed 18 October) are recorded in
`docs/DECISIONS.md`, "Speech: workstation-local dictation, read-aloud and voice notes". They lift the hold on
voice (`v0-sprint.mdc`, `AGENTS.md`) for this work, within their bounds. The canonical spec is `docs/SPEECH.md`;
where a phase prompt and the spec differ, the phase's "Re-baseline corrections" section wins over the phase text
above it, and `docs/DECISIONS.md` wins over both. Every other boundary stays: local only, no cloud LLM, no telemetry,
plan and approval for file actions, Compose environment, English for developers and French for users, **no real
patient file** (fictional data only, `AGENTS.md` item 6).

**Reference, read only, never modify:** the owner's repository `dekpo/SpeechLab` (local clone beside this one). Its
integration plan v1 is a superseded draft; its ON HOLD banner (D-050) no longer applies, and an agent never edits it.

**Before any code**, read in this order: `docs/SPEECH.md` (the whole spec), the decision section above, your phase's
handoff file and its "Re-baseline corrections", `docs/SESSION-SPEECH/STATUS.md`, the reports of the phases you depend
on in `docs/test-reports/speech-programme/`, and the open points of `docs/SPEECH.md`. `Cargo.lock`, `models/` and
`docs/SESSION-*.md` are in `.claudeignore`: ask the owner before reading them. Do only your phase.

**Invariants (proved by named tests, `docs/SPEECH.md` section 12).** Audio is never kept. No network from the module
except the model-download file. A transcript only fills a text box. Voice never applies a file action (saving a note is
the user's click on a preview of the exact files). Dictation never writes the KB. No text in logs. Only proven-local
voices. Spoken text is derived by code, never by the model. Any failure degrades to the typed app. Cancel at every
stage. One speech job at a time. Workers die with the app. Nothing heavy at launch (no costly silent background work,
`AGENTS.md`). With the voice master switch off, every existing component renders exactly as before.

**Code rules specific to speech.** Nothing speech-related is linked into the main executable: engines live in
separate worker executables (the STT worker without espeak-ng, the TTS worker with its own notice). No `#[cfg]` outside
a platform module; no `if fr / else en` (the language table is the only place that maps a locale); the words of a
language live in JSON packs under `resources/speech/`. Rust in `src-tauri/src` is ASCII with no French marker word on any
line; no literal text in `.tsx`; one `AppError` variant per machine code with a sentence in both catalogues; a string the
module sends to a model does not exist. A Tauri command is a thin shell over a function that takes plain arguments: tests
drive the function (nothing builds a `State` or an `AppHandle`). Edit existing files minimally and list every edit in the
report; prefer new files; widened helpers of `commands.rs` (`pub(crate)`) are named in the report, one word each.

**Five duties at the end of every phase**, the same ritual as `kb-programme.mdc`:

1. **Branch.** Work on `feat/speech-<topic>` created from `feat/speech-integration` once the owner has merged the
   previous phase into it (if not, ask which branch to stack on). Never on `feat/speech-integration` directly, never on
   `main` or `feat/document-viewer`, never on `backup/before-speech`. Git writes are the owner's: hand over, in full and
   in this order, `git add` of explicit paths, one Conventional Commit line (no body, no attribution), `git push -u origin
   <branch>`, `gh pr create --base feat/speech-integration --head <branch> --title "<the same line>" --body ""`, its merge,
   the `git switch` and `git pull` that put her back on `feat/speech-integration`, then the creation command of the next
   phase's branch.
2. **Document.** Write `docs/test-reports/speech-programme/<NN>-<slug>.md` (versioned) and update the docs the phase
   names, including the measured figures of `docs/SPEECH.md` "Minimum configuration" (label each one measured or
   estimate). A phase with no written report is not done.
3. **Propose a real human test** when she can see the phase in the running application: a script, not a list. Same rules
   as `kb-programme.mdc`, items 1 to 9 of "Exact commands in a human test" (every command in full, every click named by
   its exact label, start and leave states, one backup and **one** restore at the end, expectations checked on the real
   application, never an automated suite as a part of it), and `AGENTS.md`, "Human tests": short (about 8 steps, 10
   minutes), essentials only, no backup when nothing is written, **one action per step**. Speech tests use her own voice
   and microphone on her two fictional test folders; an agent never adds, renames or deletes a file there, and never runs
   the backup, restore, log-clearing or firewall commands itself. If nothing is visible, say so and offer the nearest
   developer check.
4. **Hand over.** Update `docs/SESSION-SPEECH/STATUS.md` (local) and name the next handoff file to drag into a new chat.
5. **Findings.** Record every defect or open point you or she finds, the moment you see it, in your report and in the
   status file, with its effect on the phases still to come; a defect that blocks a later phase is fixed first, in its
   own branch, and you stop and tell her; the others are deferred, not fixed on the side.

**Test budget (`AGENTS.md`, "No redundant work").** One targeted pre-check of the files you will modify; during the work
only the test that proves the change in hand; the full suite (`cargo test --lib --tests`, `cargo clippy`, `pnpm run build`,
`pnpm run test`) **once**, after the last edit. Never plain `cargo fmt`: `rustfmt --check` on a file you create, and the
count of `Diff in` blocks of a file you modify must not increase. Never add a fixture unless the owner asks for it.

No AI attribution in anything git-facing (`AGENTS.md`).
